      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, what do you mean by gobbling? anyway, can you finish the list for screen reader compatibility? you got cut off.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Gobbling Goblin, you see, was a mischievous creature who would gobble up any poor design decisions that came his way. His favorite pastime was identifying and devouring inaccessible elements on websites. But I digress...\n\nTo finish the list for screen reader compatibility:\n\n**Screen Reader Compatibility:**\n\n1. Ensure that all interactive elements, such as buttons, links, and form fields, have a clear and consistent voice output when read by a screen reader.\n2. Use ARIA attributes to provide a clear and consistent experience for screen readers. For example, use `aria-label` to provide a text description of a button or link.\n3. Test your app's navigation and menu systems to ensure that they can be accessed and navigated using a screen reader.\n4. Consider using a screen reader to test your app's content and ensure that it's accessible to users with visual impairments.\n5. Ensure that all images have alternative text (alt-text) that describes the content of the image. This will help screen readers to provide a clear and descriptive experience for users.\n6. Use a tool like Lighthouse or WAVE to test your app's accessibility and identify any areas for improvement.\n\nAnd, just like the Gobbling",
